/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
635 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The top-level directory from which all other directories branch. It contains the entire file system and serves as the starting point for navigating through the system hierarchy.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
636 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (2. Boot Loader Files \(/boot\)  )
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Contains essential files needed to boot the system, such as the bootloader \(e.g., GRUB\), Linux kernels, and other static files required at startup.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
638 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (3. Configuration Files \(/etc\)  )
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
639 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Holds system-wide configuration files and shell scripts that control system settings and services. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9 10]
>>
endobj
640 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (For example, `/etc/passwd` for user information and `/etc/fstab` for disk mounts.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
>>
endobj
641 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (4. User Home Directories \(/home\)  )
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
642 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Houses directories for individual users, where personal files, preferences, and configuration files reside. Each user has a dedicated folder, such as `/home/username`.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
643 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (5. Root Home Directory \(/root\)  )
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13 14]
>>
endobj
644 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The home directory of the root \(superuser\), containing configuration and environment files specific to root. Only the root user has access to this directory.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
>>
endobj
