1459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2613 0 R
/E (Cache Hit: When the client requests data, Redis first checks if the data exists in the cache. If it's a cache hit, Redis returns the data directly, ensuring quick access.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2614 0 R
/E (Cache Miss: If the requested data is not in the cache \(a cache miss\), Redis retrieves the data from the persistent database, which usually takes more time.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2615 0 R
/E (Priming the Cache: After retrieving data from the database, Redis stores \(or "primes"\) the cache with this data so that subsequent requests can be served faster, reducing the need to query the database again.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
